## Brief Overview

---

Technical support for events, at Malaga Event Gear, means an on site crew running your equipment through four phases: calibration before your guests arrive, live monitoring while your event runs, troubleshooting the moment something goes wrong, and teardown once it ends. Coverage across Malaga and the wider Costa del Sol.

This guide walks through what our crew actually does in each phase, the real equipment they manage on a typical booking, and how coverage changes for a wedding, a board meeting, or a two day corporate conference. [Contact us](/contact/) to confirm the coverage level for your date.

## Key Highlights

---

-   Technical support runs in four phases: calibration, live monitoring, troubleshooting, and teardown, not a single generic "technician included" line.

-   Coverage is not a yes or no question: the [Eco Pack](/packages/eco/) has none, the [Basic MICE Pack](/packages/basic-mice/) offers it as an add on, and the [Wedding Pack](/packages/wedding/) and [MICE Pack](/packages/mice/) include it as standard.

-   Our crew troubleshoots named, real equipment: mixing consoles, DMX lighting, projector and screen sync, wireless microphones, fog machines and staging, not a generic "AV system."

-   Multi day corporate events get continuous monitoring from a control panel, not a technician who checks in occasionally.

-   On packages with multiple presentation sources, a 9 input video scaler lets our technician switch between them without a visible cut. This is strictly a presentation tool: we do not offer camera systems, video recording, live streaming or broadcast equipment.

## What Actually Happens During Your Event: A Phase by Phase Look

None of the technical support providers we compared against in Malaga and Marbella describe their service this way, most stop at a single vague line about "sound checks and crew." Here is what actually happens, phase by phase, on a booking that includes it.

### Before Your Event Starts: Calibration and Testing

Our crew arrives ahead of your first guest and runs gain staging across every microphone, checks projector and screen sync, and confirms every DMX lighting cue fires as programmed. This is where a microphone that would feed back, or a projector that would not sync, gets caught and fixed before anyone notices it was ever a problem.

### While Your Event Runs: Live Monitoring

Once your event is live, the technician stays on the levels: adjusting gain as different speakers take the microphone, managing the handoff between one speaker sitting down and the next standing up so there is no dead air, and watching the video and lighting signal chain continuously rather than checking in every so often.

<InlineCTA />

### When Something Goes Wrong: Troubleshooting in Real Time

A dropped wireless microphone connection or a projector that loses sync mid presentation gets fixed on the spot, not diagnosed after your event has already suffered for it. See [Real Equipment, Real Troubleshooting](#real-equipment-real-troubleshooting-what-our-technicians-actually-fix) below for the specific gear our crew is trained on.

### After Your Event Ends: Teardown and Collection

Once your last guest has left, our crew handles rapid teardown and collection of the equipment. You never pack anything up yourself, and on a multi day booking the same crew resets and recalibrates for the next day rather than starting from zero each morning.

